Instruction: Review the following segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by selecting the option that accurately reflects the agreement.

Question: According to the merger agreement below, is the 'ability to consummate' the transaction subject to the carveouts provided under the Material Adverse Condition clause?

Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Merger Agreement Segment:
"'Material Adverse Condition' means any event, change, or circumstance that, whether occurring alone or in combination with other events, would reasonably be expected to (a) materially impair the financial condition, operational performance, or strategic position of the Company, or (b) prevent or delay beyond the stipulated closing date the consummation of the Transactions. Notwithstanding the foregoing, for purposes of determining a Material Adverse Condition, the following are expressly excluded: 
   (i) any effects arising from general economic downturns or shifts in global market conditions that affect all companies within the industry comparably;
   (ii) fluctuations in interest, credit, or foreign exchange rates that are not unique to the Company;
   (iii) delays in closing of not more than thirty (30) days, provided such delays are attributable solely to uniform regulatory changes or standard market adjustments affecting the industry at large;
   (iv) any adverse effects solely due to public announcements of this Agreement.
Further, the term 'ability to consummate' shall encompass any delay or impairment in closing resulting from a Material Adverse Condition, except where such delay or impairment is solely attributable to one or more of the exclusions enumerated herein."
