Merger Agreement Provision:

“Material Adverse Impact” means any change, event, or condition, whether occurring individually or in combination with others, that (a) materially impairs, delays, or prevents the Acquiring Party from completing the Transaction, or (b) causes a significant deterioration in its operational capacity. Notwithstanding the broad list of exceptions set forth in Sections 3.2 and 4.5—which exclude adverse effects stemming from general economic downturns, industry-wide market volatility, routine changes in accounting standards, or other factors not unique to the Acquiring Party’s operations—no such exception shall be applied in assessing any factor that directly affects the Acquiring Party’s inherent operational ability to consummate the Transaction. In other words, even if an adverse event falls under one of the generic carveout categories, it will not be excluded if it directly hinders the core capacity to execute the Transaction.
