Instruction: Read the following segment from a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.

Question: Is the “ability to consummate” concept subject to the carveouts for Material Adverse Impact as defined in this Agreement?
Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Merger Agreement: “Material Adverse Impact” means any occurrence, change, development, or effect that: (1) either directly prevents, materially delays, or substantially impairs the Company’s ability to consummate the Transaction, or adversely affects the business, assets, liabilities, or financial condition of the Company; and (2) is assessed on an aggregate basis in light of all events affecting the Company. Notwithstanding the foregoing, the following shall not be taken into account when determining whether a Material Adverse Impact has occurred:
   (i) any impact attributable to general economic or market fluctuations affecting the industry as a whole;
   (ii) effects of regulatory, legislative, or judicial changes that uniformly impact all market participants;
   (iii) adverse effects arising solely from shifts in public perception due to the announcement or publicity of this Agreement;
   (iv) any event whose impact on the consummation of the Transaction is deemed remediable or subject to renegotiation, such that its effect remains isolated from broader adverse impacts.
Furthermore, it is expressly provided that any factors affecting solely the ability to consummate—where such impact can be remedied or restructured without impairing the overall Transaction—shall not qualify for inclusion under the Material Adverse Impact assessment and are thereby excluded from the benefit of the carveouts noted above.

After carefully reviewing the Agreement, please answer the question.
